<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Best',href:'/best/'},{label:'Sell Digital Products'}]}/><article><header class="section"><div class="container"><h1>Best Platforms to Sell Digital Products in 2026: Product Type Changes the Economics</h1><p class="lede">A $10 download, $100 course, $20 subscription and $500 software license should not automatically use the same platform.</p></div></header><div class="narrow prose article-body"><AffiliateDisclosure hasRelationship={true}/><h2>Best fits</h2><p><strong>Payhip</strong> for transparent creator-commerce fees; <strong>Sellfy</strong> for a fixed-subscription branded store; <strong>Gumroad</strong> for zero monthly commitment and Merchant of Record simplicity; <strong>Lemon Squeezy</strong> for software/subscriptions with Merchant of Record; <strong>Podia</strong> for courses/downloads/community; <strong>Systeme.io</strong> when selling must connect to funnels, email and automation.</p><h2>Why average order value matters</h2><p>Fixed per-transaction charges hit a $10 download harder than a $500 product. Percentage fees scale with revenue. Fixed subscriptions do the opposite: painful at zero revenue, increasingly small as a share of sales. That is why “cheapest platform” is not a stable label.</p><h2>Tax model matters too</h2><p>Gumroad and Lemon Squeezy operate as Merchant of Record. Payhip automatically handles EU/UK VAT but should not be treated as the same global legal model. Compare responsibility, not just percentages.</p><h2>Verdict</h2><p>Payhip is our first comparison for general creator products because its fee ladder is transparent. Lemon Squeezy is stronger for software-oriented MoR needs. Systeme.io is more compelling when commerce is one part of a marketing stack.</p><p><a class="button" href={aff} rel="sponsored nofollow">See Systeme.io's Free plan</a></p><SourceBlock sources={sources}/><p class="source-note">We merged the near-duplicate “best digital product platform” intent into this page rather than creating a competing URL. Facts checked September 10, 2026.</p></div></article></BaseLayout>